        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Declaration Template\n\nA draft of what a new graph looks like.\n\nOpen the page view for the tutorial: each part of a Declaration, and how to build one. This graph is laid out the way a graph should be, so it is worth reading as well as running."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "dt-detail",
      "type": "face",
      "label": "Page",
      "position": {
        "x": -1500,
        "y": 1340
      },
      "width": 760,
      "height": 507,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270
        }
      ],
      "handles": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root"
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "declaration-template"
        },
        "view": {
          "intent": "node",
          "payload": "node.web.detail",
          "scroll": "page"
        },
        "semanticLevel": "detail",
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='width:100%;height:100%;overflow:auto;box-sizing:border-box;container-type:inline-size;background:#ffffff;font-family:Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#0f172a'><div style='box-sizing:border-box;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 clamp(20px,5cqw,40px) 64px'><div style='padding-top:32px'><header data-role='masthead'><div style='padding:10px 0;border-top:3px solid #0f172a;border-bottom:1px solid #0f172a;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>TWILITE · NEW GRAPH</div><h1 style='margin:40px 0 0;font:500 clamp(40px,9.5cqw,72px)/.98 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.025em;text-wrap:balance;color:#0f172a'>Your Declaration</h1><p style='margin:18px 0 0;max-width:36ch;font:italic 400 clamp(20px,3.6cqw,26px)/1.35 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#475569'>Every graph starts here: who it is, how it shows itself, and where a visitor lands. Read the page, then build yours the same way.</p></header></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:flex-start;gap:32px 40px'><div style='flex:999 1 380px;min-width:0'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>What a Declaration is</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><span style='float:left;padding:6px 10px 0 0;font:500 68px/54px Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#0f172a'>A</span> Declaration is the one node that gives a graph its identity. It says what the graph is called, what kind of thing it is, how it presents itself at every size, and what it lets other graphs use.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>It is also the graph&#39;s root. Visitors and other graphs reach everything through it, so you never add a separate root node. A graph without a Declaration is only a fragment: content that has not been given an identity yet.</p></section></div><div style='flex:1 1 220px;min-width:0'><section data-role='settings' style='padding-top:10px;border-top:3px solid #0f172a'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0f172a'>The parts at a glance</h2><dl style='margin:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:max-content 1fr;column-gap:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #cbd5e1'><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Identity</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>A name, a version, a description, and a stable id</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Intent</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>What kind of graph this is, and who it is for</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Surface</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>A chain of Views, richest first, ending in a Glyph</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Ground</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>Where a visitor lands, and the frame of the graph</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Ports</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>Optional named entry points other graphs can use</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Authority</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>Who may create, change or delete: people, agents, tools</dd></dl></section></div></div></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>1. Name it</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Open the Declaration&#39;s editor and fill in the Identity tab: a name people will recognize, a version, and a one-sentence description. The description shows on cards and in lists, so write it for someone who has never seen the graph.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>The graph&#39;s id is made for you and must stay the same for the life of the graph, because other graphs find yours by it. Rename the graph freely. Never change its id.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>2. Say what it is</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Choose the kind of graph: a plan, a note, a contract, a task board, a template. The kind belongs on the Declaration, never in a title node or a field. It tells people, and tools, how to treat the graph.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Keep the scope honest too: local for your own work, shared for something others will build on.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>3. Give it a face</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>A graph presents itself through a Surface: a chain of Views, richest first, ending in a Glyph. Drag from the surface handle on the Declaration to make the first View. Drag from that View&#39;s next-view handle to add a smaller one. Add a Glyph last and connect it, so the chain ends in a mark that still reads at a tiny size.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>One View is enough to begin. Twilite shows the richest View that fits the room it has, and the Glyph when there is almost none. This page is exactly that: a page, a summary card, an icon, then a glyph.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>4. Choose where it lands</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Every graph has one landing surface: the node a visitor sees first. Drag from the landing surface handle to create it.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Its distance and direction from the Declaration also set the graph&#39;s frame, the shape it takes as a card in a workspace. Size it so everything that should stay visible is inside the frame.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>5. Decide what you share</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Ports are optional named entry points that other graphs can use. Leave them out until you need one.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Bridges bring in the node classes you build with (imports, down the left of this graph) and offer yours to others (exports, top right). Authority says who may create, change or delete: people, agents, tools. Anything not granted is not allowed.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>6. Lay it out</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Put each kind of node in its own place so the graph reads at a glance. The Declaration goes top left. Import bridges run down the left side. Export bridges take the top half of the right side and portals the bottom half. Views run along the bottom, and everything else stays inside that boundary.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Look at this graph: that is where everything is. An empty region is fine. The layout is about where things sit, not about filling every corner.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>7. Check it</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Open the graph and look for warnings. A missing landing surface, a Surface that does not end in a Glyph, and two Surfaces with the same id are all reported, and each names the node to fix.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Then save, close and reopen it. A graph that looks the same after reloading is sound.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:40px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:stretch;gap:28px 32px'><div style='flex:1 1 190px;min-width:0'><section data-role='route' data-prominence='primary' style='height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;display:flex;flex-direction:column;padding-top:12px;border-top:3px solid #6d28d9'><h2 style='margin:0 0 8px;font:500 26px/1.1 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#0f172a'>Read the Declaration primitive</h2><p style='margin:0 0 10px;font:400 14px/1.55 system-ui;color:#475569'>The full specification: what it means, what makes it a Declaration, its data, its relationships, and what it is not.</p><a data-role='route-link' href='tlz://twilite.zone/library/primitives/root/root.node' style='margin-top:auto;font:700 13px/20px system-ui;text-decoration:none;color:#6d28d9'>Open the primitive →</a></section></div><div style='flex:1 1 190px;min-width:0'><section data-role='route' data-prominence='secondary' style='height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;display:flex;flex-direction:column;padding-top:14px;border-top:1px solid #0f172a'><h2 style='margin:0 0 8px;font:500 22px/1.1 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#0f172a'>Read the Surface primitive</h2><p style='margin:0 0 10px;font:400 14px/1.55 system-ui;color:#475569'>How a graph presents itself: chains of Views that end in a Glyph.</p><a data-role='route-link' href='tlz://twilite.zone/library/primitives/surface/root.node' style='margin-top:auto;font:700 13px/20px system-ui;text-decoration:none;color:#475569'>Open the primitive →</a></section></div></div></div></div></div>"
        }
      }
    },
